E7 gate — the deferred teardown, observed live (the self-kill arm). 2026-11-02.
image:    sls_operating_system_e3_selfkill.iso — make x86-iso-e3
          E3_INIT_FEATURES=e3_envs,e7_gate E7_SELFKILL=1 (the only build carrying
          the gate stop's self-kill arm; every other build compiles it out)
machine:  qemu-system-x86_64 -m 1G -smp 2 -accel tcg,thread=multi, 10 GiB NVMe
invocation: E7G_ARTIFACTS=/tmp/e7-selfkill-run tests/e7_gate_check.sh --selfkill
result:   rc=0 — all five clauses green.

Why this case exists: process_kill() defers a RUNNING target and the scheduler
tears it down on its next pass, but every other gate run kills a parked task, so
that branch had only ever been compiled. Here the stop — the one instant a
poisoned Linux task is provably RUNNING in its own trap — asks for the running
tenant's death. No checkpoint is taken.

=== the transcript (boot1.log lines 2069-2079) ===
2069  [E7-GATE] stack hwm (stop): alloc=65536 poison-span=60912 headroom=58264 deepest=2648 lowest=0x4000000a9398
2070  [E7-GATE] self-kill: process_kill(pid=105) requested while RUNNING
2071  [PROC] PID 105 terminated.
2072  [PROC] PID 105 kill deferred (running) — teardown at next schedule
2078  [PROC] PID 105 'e7.gate.user.0' deferred teardown (killed while running)
2079  [E7-GATE] stack hwm (exit): alloc=65536 poison-span=60912 headroom=58264 deepest=2648 lowest=0x4000000a9398

=== the clauses ===
S1 ok — the kill was requested while the tenant was RUNNING and process_kill()
        deferred it (self-kill: process_kill(pid=105)).
S2 ok — the scheduler's deferred branch tore the tenant down
        ([PROC] PID 105 'e7.gate.user.0' deferred teardown).
S3 ok — exactly one stack scan, and it follows the deferred-teardown line
        (line 2079 after 2078): the scan happens at the teardown, not at the
        request.
S4 ok — the deferred scan re-measures the same stack: deepest 2648 below the
        entry %rsp (2648 at the stop), 58264 of 65536 untouched — 88.9% above
        the 32768-byte floor. The poisoned span is the stop's 60912.
S5 ok — no [backup] Done: the tenant was taken down mid-copy.

Between lines 2072 and 2078 the tenant ran on (the arm returns to the sysret),
which is what makes this the deferred case rather than an immediate kill; the
scheduler caught it with the pending flag and swept it. The sweep is part of the
change: a deferred kill is a property of the kill, not of which task the timer
happened to interrupt, so a target that parks before the tick is torn down on
the next pass too.

Recorded from the first passing run; an earlier attempt failed in the harness
itself (the probe's local initializer read an unset variable), not in the
kernel.
